WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:15.868
Every balance in the books goes to one of two places. Income and expenses for the year go to the income statement. What the business owns or owes at the year end goes to the statement of financial position.

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:29.270
Assets are split into non-current, kept and used for more than a year, and current, which turn into cash soon. Liabilities are current if due within twelve months, and non-current if due later.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:49.329
Ask three questions. Is it income or an expense for the year? Then it goes to the income statement. Is it owned? Used for over a year means non-current. Is it owed? Due within a year means current.

00:00:49.900 --> 00:01:03.271
Spending follows the same rule. Buying a van is capital expenditure: the van is an asset in the statement of financial position. Its fuel and repairs are revenue expenditure: expenses in the income statement.

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:21.170
Two items catch students out. Accumulated depreciation reduces the asset, so it sits with non-current assets. The provision for irrecoverable debts reduces trade receivables, so it sits under current assets. Neither is a liability.

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:40.142
Here is a real question from January 2020. Jeff's year ends on thirty-first December 2019. Tick where each of six items is shown in the statement of financial position. Six marks, one for each row.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:53.634
Accumulated depreciation reduces the machinery, so it goes under non-current assets. The bank loan is repayable in 2026, more than a year after the year end, so it is a non-current liability.

00:01:54.200 --> 00:02:06.152
Work in progress is goods partly made: it is part of inventory, so a current asset. A bank overdraft can be called in at any time, so it is a current liability.

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:23.812
Other receivables, such as expenses paid in advance, are short-term amounts due to the business: current assets. The provision for irrecoverable debts comes off trade receivables, so it is under current assets too. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:34.596
A common mistake is calling the provision a liability. It is a deduction from trade receivables, so it belongs with current assets.

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:45.261
Another is putting irrecoverable debts written off in the statement of financial position. A debt written off is an expense of the year, so it goes to the income statement.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:59.398
Now you try. A florist's year ends on thirty-first March 2026. Decide where each item goes. Pause and decide.

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:14.170
The van is a non-current asset, and petty cash is a current asset. The loan is due within a year, so it is a current liability, and so is the rent owing. The debt written off is an expense in the income statement.

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:24.384
Remember: expense for the year, or owned, or owed? Then ask: within a year, or longer? Now try the practice questions.
